1 CONC Here is a comprehensive chronicle of the life of Louis (Lui
1 CONC gi) Pittaluga, tracking his 83-year journey from northern I
1 CONC taly to the farmlands of Pennsylvania, and finally to the c
1 CONC oast of Florida.ðŸ®ðŸ¹ Early Life and Origins in Italy (189
1 CONC 8–1927)Birth: Luigi Pittaluga was born on October 1, 1898
1 CONC , in the historic maritime city of Genoa (Genova) in the Li
1 CONC guria region of Italy. His surname was deeply rooted in th
1 CONC e local grape-harvesting traditions of the area.Family Stru
1 CONC cture: He grew up in a large household with at least five s
1 CONC iblings, including his sister Luigina and his brother Angel
1 CONC o.Coming of Age: His youth was defined by the industrial ex
1 CONC pansion of Genoa and the turmoil of World War I. Coming o
1 CONC f age during the war, Luigi belonged to the historic "Ragaz
1 CONC zi del '99" (The Boys of '99) generation, making him subjec
1 CONC t to mandatory conscription into the Italian Royal Army (Re
1 CONC gio Esercito) during the height of the conflict.The Decisio
1 CONC n to Leave: Following the war, Italy faced severe economi
1 CONC c depression and the rise of fascism under Mussolini. Facin
1 CONC g limited economic opportunities, the 28-year-old Luigi dec
1 CONC ided to look across the Atlantic for a new beginning.ðŸš¢ T
1 CONC he Transatlantic Transition (1927–1929)The Canadian Gateway
1 CONC : Due to highly restrictive U.S. immigration quotas enacte
1 CONC d in 1924, Luigi bypassed direct entry to America. In 1927
1 CONC , he boarded an ocean liner out of Genoa or Naples and immi
1 CONC grated to Canada, landing at a major gateway like Pier 21 i
1 CONC n Halifax or the Port of Quebec.Crossing the Border: Afte
1 CONC r living and working transiently in Canada for two years, h
1 CONC e secured legal passage into the United States. In 1929, a
1 CONC t age 30, he officially entered America through the land-bo
1 CONC rder inspection gates of the St. Albans District (likely cr
1 CONC ossing at Buffalo, NY, or Detroit, MI), listing Philadelphi
1 CONC a, Pennsylvania, as his final destination.ðŸ§± Building a F
1 CONC amily in Philadelphia (1930–1940s)Marriage: Settling into t
1 CONC he industrial enclaves of North Philadelphia, he met Angeli
1 CONC na ("Angie") DeCristoforo, the daughter of Italian immigran
1 CONC ts Agostino and Luigia DeCristoforo. The couple was marrie
1 CONC d in 1934 in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania.The Wister Street H
1 CONC ousehold: By 1935, the couple was renting a home at 211 Wis
1 CONC ter Street in Philadelphia's 12th Ward. The 1940 U.S. Censu
1 CONC s captures Louis working steadily as a mason and bricklaye
1 CONC r in the local construction industry to support his growin
1 CONC g family.Children: Together, Louis and Angelina raised thre
1 CONC e children, naming them in accordance with traditional Ital
1 CONC ian family customs:Augustine Luigi Emmanuel Pittaluga (Bor
1 CONC n August 2, 1935; named after his maternal grandfather, Ago
1 CONC stino)Joseph Robert Pittaluga (Born September 19, 1937; nam
1 CONC ed after his maternal uncle)Louise Pittaluga (Born circa 19
1 CONC 39; named after her maternal grandmother, Luigia)ðŸ¡ Innov
1 CONC ation at Bella Vista Farm (Late 1940s–1950s)The Country Est
1 CONC ate: Seeking a more rural life for his children, Louis use
1 CONC d his masonry earnings to purchase a property in Montgomer
1 CONC y County, Pennsylvania, known as "Bella Vista Farm", locate
1 CONC d in the Willow Grove area (Upper Moreland Township).Life o
1 CONC n the Farm: The 1950 U.S. Census shows the family completel
1 CONC y settled on the farm. While Louis managed the agricultura
1 CONC l land, he continued to leverage his bricklaying skills fo
1 CONC r regional contracting work. His sons grew up here, attendi
1 CONC ng local schools (Upper Moreland or Hatboro-Horsham High) u
1 CONC ntil graduating in 1953 (Augustine) and 1955 (Joseph).The I
1 CONC nventor: Louis possessed a highly mechanical mind. While li
1 CONC ving on the farm, he designed a specialized structural cast
1 CONC ing and filed a framework design with the government. On De
1 CONC cember 7, 1954, he was officially granted U.S. Patent No. 2
1 CONC ,696,396 for a "Corner Fitting for Swing Frames."ðŸŒ´ Retir
1 CONC ement in Hollywood, Florida (1960s–1981)The Move South: Loc
1 CONC al Willow Grove directories indicate the Pittalugas left Be
1 CONC lla Vista Farm around 1957 or 1958 as their children reache
1 CONC d adulthood. Seeking a warmer climate for retirement, Loui
1 CONC s and Angelina migrated south to Hollywood, Florida, a coas
1 CONC tal city in Broward County.Civic Life: By the early 1960s
1 CONC , Louis and Angelina were fully established Florida residen
1 CONC ts, consistently registering and voting as members of the D
1 CONC emocratic Party in Broward County's coastal precincts. Duri
1 CONC ng this era, they watched their sons build independent live
1 CONC s, including Joseph, who went on to serve in the U.S. milit
1 CONC ary.Passing: Louis Pittaluga passed away on October 1, 1981
1 CONC —the exact day of his 83rd birthday—in Hollywood, Florida.F
1 CONC inal Resting Place: He was entombed in Mausoleum 2 (Outside
1 CONC ) at Hollywood Memorial Gardens East Cemetery (6301 Taft St
1 CONC reet). Four years later, following her passing in 1985, hi
1 CONC s beloved wife Angelina was laid to rest right by his side.
1 CONC This complete timeline successfully preserves his legacy a
1 CONC s a determined immigrant, an skilled craftsman, a farm inno
1 CONC vator, and a dedicated family patriarch.
